import java.util.Objects;

// Invariant: min <= max.
public class Range {
    private final int min;
    private final int max;
    private final String label;

    public Range(int a, int b, String label) {          // the one real constructor.
        this.min = Math.min(a, b);                      // normalise order.
        this.max = Math.max(a, b);
        this.label = Objects.requireNonNull(label, "label");
    }

    public Range(int a, int b) { this(a, b, "range"); }   // delegates: no copied checks.

    public Range() { this(0, 0); }                      // explicit no-arg constructor.

    public boolean contains(int x) { return x >= min && x <= max; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Range r = new Range(10, 3);                     // normalised to 3..10.
        System.out.println(r.contains(5) + " " + r.min + ".." + r.max);   // true 3..10
    }
}
```

| Technique | Purpose |
|---|---|
| validate or normalise in one constructor. | invariant has one implementation. |
| `this(...)` first in others. | convenience without duplication. |
| `final` fields. | can't change after construction. |
| `Objects.requireNonNull`. | reject null early with a clear message. |

